		<wp:postmeta>
			<wp:meta_key><![CDATA[bullet_1]]></wp:meta_key>
			<wp:meta_value><![CDATA[Homeowners Who Received a Court Letter: Individuals who have been served with a foreclosure notice or summons and need to understand their legal rights.]]></wp:meta_value>
		</wp:postmeta>
		<wp:postmeta>
			<wp:meta_key><![CDATA[bullet_2]]></wp:meta_key>
			<wp:meta_value><![CDATA[Those Who Can't Afford an Attorney: Low-to-moderate income homeowners who need professional legal representation but cannot pay for private counsel.]]></wp:meta_value>
		</wp:postmeta>
		<wp:postmeta>
			<wp:meta_key><![CDATA[bullet_3]]></wp:meta_key>
			<wp:meta_value><![CDATA[People Unsure How to Respond: Homeowners who received legal paperwork and don't know what steps to take or what deadlines they're facing.]]></wp:meta_value>
		</wp:postmeta>
		<wp:postmeta>
			<wp:meta_key><![CDATA[long_description]]></wp:meta_key>
			<wp:meta_value><![CDATA[The Pennsylvania Legal Aid Network (PLAN) provides free legal assistance to homeowners dealing with foreclosure proceedings and consumer rights issues.

Case Review: A legal aid attorney reviews your foreclosure documents, court filings, and mortgage history to identify potential defenses or violations by the lender.

Court Representation: In qualifying cases, PLAN can represent you in court, file responses on your behalf, and negotiate directly with the lender's legal team.

Rights Education: PLAN explains your rights under Pennsylvania foreclosure law, including mandatory mediation requirements and notice periods that lenders must follow.

Referral Network: If your case requires specialized assistance beyond PLAN's scope, they connect you with other legal organizations and housing resources in your area.]]></wp:meta_value>
